--- a/poteto-mode/playbooks/refactoring.md
+++ b/poteto-mode/playbooks/refactoring.md
@@ -6,6 +6,25 @@
 
 1. Pin the behavior contract first. Run the **how** skill over the affected subsystem to learn the contract, then write a characterization test, snapshot, or equivalence harness that captures current behavior before any structure moves. If the area has no coverage, write the pin before touching structure. Type check and lint are not a pin.
 2. Name the structure the code is missing per **principle-model-the-domain**. Boring code stays when the shape is already clear and local. The reshape must delete branches or invalid states, not add indirection.
+   Name each smell you find by its table name and pick a listed refactoring. A smell is a reason to look, not a verdict. Leave it when no listed refactoring deletes a branch, a layer, a parameter, or a piece of state. Put the smell and refactoring names in the commit message.
+
+   | Smell | What you see | Refactorings | Leave it when |
+   |---|---|---|---|
+   | Mysterious Name | A name that needs the body read to know what it holds or does. | Change Function Declaration, Rename Variable, Rename Field | No honest name comes. Then the shape is wrong. Go to step 3. |
+   | Duplicated Code | The same statement shape in two or more places. | Extract Function, Slide Statements | The copies change for different reasons. |
+   | Long Function | A block preceded by a comment saying what it does. A conditional whose branches each run several lines. | Extract Function, Decompose Conditional, Split Loop | The extracted piece would have one caller and no name better than the comment. |
+   | Long Parameter List | Five or more params. A boolean param that picks a code path. | Introduce Parameter Object, Preserve Whole Object, Remove Flag Argument, Replace Parameter with Query | Each param is independent and every caller passes different values. |
+   | Global or Mutable Data | Module-level mutable state, a singleton, a field kept in sync with another field. | Encapsulate Variable, Replace Derived Variable with Query, Separate Query from Modifier, Split Variable | The state is a local already. |
+   | Divergent Change | One file edited in this diff for two unrelated reasons. | Split Phase, Extract Function, Move Function | The two reasons share one invariant. |
+   | Shotgun Surgery | One logical change edits many files with the same small change. | Move Function, Move Field, Combine Functions into Class, Inline Function | The files are separate deploy units. |
+   | Feature Envy | A function that reads more fields of another module's data than of its own. | Move Function, Extract Function | The data is a wire or storage type kept dumb on purpose. |
+   | Data Clumps | The same three or more fields or params travel together across signatures. | Introduce Parameter Object, Extract Class, Preserve Whole Object | They meet in only one place. |
+   | Primitive Obsession | A string or number standing for an id, unit, money, or status. | Replace Primitive with Object, Replace Type Code with Subclasses | The value never leaves one function. |
+   | Repeated Switches | The same `switch` or `if` cascade on the same tag in two or more places. | Replace Conditional with Polymorphism | Variants hold data, not behavior. Use one lookup table or discriminated union instead. |
+   | Lazy Element | A function or class whose body is one call. An abstract type with one implementation. | Inline Function, Inline Class, Collapse Hierarchy | It adds policy or adaptation. |
+   | Speculative Generality | Params, hooks, or type params no caller varies. Code whose only callers are tests. | Remove Dead Code, Change Function Declaration, Inline Function, Collapse Hierarchy | A second real caller exists in this repo. |
+   | Message Chains | `a.b().c().d()` in a caller. | Hide Delegate, Extract Function, Move Function | The chain is a fluent builder API. |
+   | Middle Man | A class where most methods forward to one other object. | Remove Middle Man, Inline Function | The forwarding adds policy. |
 3. Name the target shape. State what the module layout, types, and call graph should be if built today (**principle-foundational-thinking**, **principle-redesign-from-first-principles**). If the target crosses a function boundary, run the **architect** skill for parallel design exploration of the shape before the move.
 4. Subtract before you add. Delete dead code, collapse one-caller wrappers, drop redundant validators, and remove orphan references before introducing the new shape (**principle-subtract-before-you-add**). The smallest change that reaches the target shape ships (**principle-laziness-protocol**). A speculative cleanup that "might help" gets reverted.
 5. Move in small behavior-preserving steps, each keeping the pin green. For API reshapes, migrate every caller and delete the old API in the same wave (**principle-migrate-callers-then-delete-legacy-apis**). No compatibility shims, no parallel old-and-new paths. Spot-check every rename against the actual files. Renames silently miss usages in strings, prose, and back-references. If the repo has a GitNexus index (`.gitnexus/` exists), refresh it first (`gitnexus analyze --index-only`, incremental and a no-op when up to date; rerun it whenever a staleness notice appears mid-wave before the next graph query), then enumerate callers with `gitnexus impact <symbol>` and `gitnexus context <symbol>` before the wave — and still spot-check against the actual files: the index reflects the last analyzed commit and never sees uncommitted edits. Delegate the mechanical edits to a subagent using your configured refactoring model (default `sonnet`) with a specific scope (file paths, the names being moved, the behavior to hold). Review the diff yourself.
